LikeOS
======

A modern 64-bit operating system, written from scratch.

Its own kernel, its own drivers and its own C library -- and on top of them a
complete graphical desktop with sound, on which you can read your mail, chat,
manage files, listen to music, watch videos and browse today's web.

LikeOS is an independent operating system for 64-bit PCs.  It is not a
distribution of an existing system and not a re-skin of one: the kernel that
starts when the machine boots, the drivers that talk to the hardware and the C
library that every program is linked against were all written for LikeOS.  A
large set of unmodified upstream programs is ported on top -- from bash and
OpenSSH up to an X.Org desktop, GTK applications and a WebKit web browser.

LikeOS boots on real notebooks as well as in virtual machines, logs you in as
an ordinary user, and starts a graphical desktop with a taskbar, desktop icons,
sound and familiar applications.  Its web browser renders the same complex
sites you use every day -- maps, web shops, search, AI chat -- and, on Intel
graphics and on VMware or VirtualBox with 3D enabled, draws them with the GPU.

  Web site:     https://likeos.systemtrap.com
  Source:       https://github.com/kctherootkey/LikeOS-64
  Version:      0.2.7-HEAD
  License:      GNU General Public License, version 2

Branches: master is the stable branch.  dev is unstable, but probably has more
features; checking it out is optional.

No downloadable images yet: pre-built images will be published with the first
major release.  Until then LikeOS is built from source, as described below.

Current state
-------------

- Lenovo ThinkPad P50: tested; the reference machine.  Intel HD Graphics 530
  on the i915 driver with GPU acceleration, HD Audio sound (Realtek ALC298),
  I219 Ethernet, USB boot.
- Dell Pro 16 Plus: tested; USB boot.
- VMware: supported.  USB 3.1 boot; vmwgfx with 3D acceleration, overlay
  video, Sound Blaster PCI (es1371) sound, vmxnet3 or e1000 networking.
- VirtualBox: supported.  xHCI (USB 3) and the VMSVGA adapter, with 3D when
  enabled; ICH AC'97 sound.
- QEMU: supported through the legacy targets.  xHCI, graphics on the UEFI
  framebuffer (there is no VMware SVGA support under QEMU), Intel HD Audio
  sound; KVM recommended for the desktop.
- UEFI boot is required; there is no legacy BIOS boot path.
- LikeOS runs from a USB drive.  AHCI/SATA and NVMe are not supported yet, so
  the machine's internal disks are left untouched.

The i915 driver's device table reaches far beyond the machines it has been
verified on; real-hardware testing so far has been on the two notebooks above.

What you can do with it today
-----------------------------

- Browse the web: the luakit browser on WebKitGTK, with an ad blocker and
  filter lists for every region.
- Read and send mail: Claws Mail over IMAP, POP3 and SMTP, encrypted with TLS.
- Chat: the HexChat IRC client -- and the web's chat services in the browser.
- Manage files: PCManFM draws the desktop and manages files; Mousepad edits
  text.
- Work in a terminal: bash, tmux, OpenSSH, curl and a full set of Unix tools.
- Listen and watch: the Media Player for music and video, sound from the web,
  and a volume control in the taskbar -- on LikeOS's own sound drivers.

At a glance
-----------

  Architecture      x86-64 (long mode), higher-half kernel, up to 64 CPUs
  Boot              UEFI only; own GNU-EFI bootloader that loads the ELF64
                    kernel straight from the ext4 root filesystem
  Kernel            monolithic, preemptive, SMP; 199 system calls with a
                    POSIX-style interface; ACPI through ACPICA 20251212
  Root filesystem   ext4 (read/write, journalled), on a single GPT USB disk
                    image: FAT32 EFI System Partition + ext4 root
  Graphics          own DRM/KMS core with GEM, sync objects and DMA-BUF;
                    native Intel i915 and VMware SVGA (vmwgfx) drivers; UEFI
                    framebuffer fallback
  Sound             own drivers for Intel HD Audio (with HDMI and DisplayPort
                    audio), the Intel audio DSP with SoundWire, AC'97 and the
                    Ensoniq AudioPCI; the audiod service mixes every program
  Desktop           X.Org server 21.1 (modesetting + glamor), ctwm with a
                    Windows 11-style look, the LikeOS taskbar with a volume
                    control, PCManFM desktop, GTK 3.24, a media player
  Web browser       luakit 2.4.0 on WebKitGTK 2.52.6
  Shell             bash 5.2 as /bin/sh

Feature summary
---------------

Kernel core
~~~~~~~~~~~
The kernel is organised into subsystems: the executive (ke), the hardware
abstraction layer (hal), memory management (mm), filesystems (fs), console and
terminals (io), device drivers (dev) and networking (net).

- 64-bit long mode, higher-half mapping, four-level paging.
- UEFI boot path with framebuffer (GOP) and memory-map handoff.
- Up to 64 CPUs; AP start-up via INIT-SIPI-SIPI and a real-mode trampoline.
- IDT, Local APIC (xAPIC and x2APIC), IOAPIC routing, PCI MSI.
- SMP with per-CPU data via the GS base, per-CPU run queues, IPIs and TLB
  shootdown.
- 199 system calls, entered through the SYSCALL instruction.
- Per-task extended processor state (x87/SSE/AVX through XSAVE), saved and
  restored across context switches and in signal frames.
- Timekeeping: LAPIC timer tick, TSC-based precise time, and high-resolution
  timers on an HPET comparator, giving precise nanosleep, clock_nanosleep,
  futex and poll timeouts.
- Clocks: CLOCK_REALTIME, MONOTONIC, MONOTONIC_RAW, BOOTTIME, the coarse
  variants and per-process/per-thread CPU-time clocks.  POSIX interval timers
  (timer_create family), setitimer and alarm.
- CPU performance: HWP with the energy-performance preference set to maximum
  performance on each core, and Enhanced SpeedStep on older Intel CPUs.
- ACPI through ACPICA 20251212: table parsing, AML interpreter, namespace
  enumeration; S5 poweroff and the ACPI reset register.
- Firmware loader that reads blobs (Intel GPU DMC, GuC and HuC images) from
  /lib/firmware.
- ChaCha20-based CSPRNG with an entropy pool, /dev/random, /dev/urandom and
  getrandom.
- Assertion framework (BUG_ON, WARN_ON, WARN_ON_ONCE, VM_BUG_ON, BUILD_BUG_ON)
  and full crash dumps for kernel and user faults.

Console and terminals
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
- Framebuffer console with double buffering, write-combining, scrollback, a
  scrollbar and a mouse pointer.
- Virtual terminals with an ANSI/VT100/VT220/xterm emulator: 256-colour and
  true-colour SGR, scroll regions, alternate screen, mouse reporting and
  terminal query replies, so full-screen programs work.
- POSIX TTY line discipline, job-control signals, and pseudo-terminals
  (/dev/ptmx, /dev/pts) -- what tmux, xterm and sshd sessions run on.
- UTF-8 end to end: the console decodes it and addresses glyphs by code point,
  the terminal emulator tracks character widths, and the C library implements
  the full multibyte and wide-character interface.  The console font is a PSF
  font (Terminus) with a Unicode table.
- Serial and USB-serial logging back ends.

Memory management
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
- Physical page allocator with a bitmap and per-page reference counts; a
  RAM-sized direct map.
- SLAB allocator for kernel objects; memory poisoning in debug builds.
- Demand paging: anonymous memory, brk, BSS and file-backed mappings are
  populated on first touch, not at exec.
- Copy-on-write fork, plus a real vfork that shares the address space.
- mmap/munmap (anonymous, private and shared file mappings), mprotect, mremap,
  madvise, mincore, and brk that returns memory when the heap shrinks.
- A per-process mapping table that grows on demand, up to 65,530 mappings --
  enough for large GTK and WebKit processes.
- Guard-paged stacks; no-execute (NX) mappings, with kernel sections remapped
  by permission.
- System V shared memory and semaphores, POSIX shared memory (/dev/shm) and
  memfd_create.
- Unified page cache with read-ahead and CLOCK eviction, shared by ext4 and
  FAT32.
- OOM killer: when reclaim cannot satisfy an allocation, a victim process is
  chosen and killed instead of the machine stalling.

Scheduling and processes
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
- Preemptive, fair scheduler: every task is charged the CPU time it uses
  (vruntime), and the task that has run least runs next.  Per-CPU run queues
  kept in vruntime order; woken tasks are placed just ahead of the runnable
  ones.
- Periodic load balancing between CPUs, and CPU affinity
  (sched_setaffinity/sched_getaffinity).  The standard sched_* calls are
  accepted; there are no priority classes.
- fork, vfork, clone (threads, TLS, child TID set/clear, shared files, FS and
  signal handlers), execve, exit_group, wait4.
- ELF64 loading of static and dynamically linked programs, and #! scripts.
- Process groups, sessions and job control.
- Signals: rt_sigaction with SA_SIGINFO, real-time queued signals,
  sigaltstack, sigsuspend, sigtimedwait, tgkill, and syscall restart.
- Futexes: wait/wake, requeue and compare-requeue, wake-op, bitsets,
  priority-inheritance locks, and robust lists; process-private and shared
  keys.
- Event interfaces: poll, ppoll, select, pselect6, epoll, eventfd, timerfd,
  signalfd, pipes.
- ptrace: attach/trace-me, peek/poke memory and user area, general and FP
  registers, single-step, syscall tracing, fork/vfork/clone/exec/exit events,
  and hardware watchpoints through the debug registers.
- /proc: self, <pid>/maps, cmdline, comm, exe, cwd, fd, stat, statm, status,
  auxv, mem, plus cpuinfo, meminfo, mounts, uptime, version.

Filesystems and storage
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
- ext4 is the root filesystem, with full read and write support: extent trees
  (with growth) and indirect block mapping, hash-indexed (htree) directories
  including directory hashing on write, a JBD2 journal in ordered-data mode
  with replay after an unclean shutdown, 64-bit, flex_bg and metadata_csum
  (CRC32C) filesystems, extended attributes, symbolic and hard links, and
  fallocate.
- Correct unlink-while-open semantics: an orphaned inode lives until its last
  close.
- The bootloader reads /boot/kernel.elf straight out of ext4.
- FAT32 with long file names, used for the EFI System Partition.
- tmpfs mounted at /ram, with hard links (used to run the browser's cache in
  RAM).
- devfs: /dev/null, zero, random, urandom, tty, console, ptmx, pts/*, fb0,
  input/event*, dri/card*, dri/renderD*, shm, fd and the disk nodes.
- procfs (/proc) and sysfs (/sys/bus/pci/devices and device classes such as
  the backlight).
- VFS with symlinks, hard links, *at() calls, chroot, statfs, utimensat,
  sync/fsync/fdatasync, and POSIX advisory record locks
  (F_GETLK/F_SETLK/F_SETLKW).
- Dentry cache (positive and negative entries), inode cache, and the unified
  page cache with dirty writeback.
- Block device layer over USB mass storage (Bulk-Only Transport) on xHCI, with
  1 MiB write coalescing for USB throughput.
- AHCI/SATA and NVMe controllers are not yet supported, and mounting removable
  media is still pending.

Devices and buses
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
- PCI: configuration-space enumeration, capabilities, MSI; exported in
  /sys/bus/pci.
- ACPI: ACPICA 20251212 -- tables (RSDP/XSDT/MADT/FADT), AML, device
  enumeration, S5 poweroff, reset.
- Interrupt controllers: Local APIC (xAPIC and x2APIC), IOAPIC; HPET.
- USB: xHCI (USB 3.x) host controller driver, interrupt-driven; mass storage
  (Bulk-Only Transport); HID boot keyboards and mice, report-protocol mice with
  scroll wheel; USB-serial logging.
- Legacy input: PS/2 keyboard and mouse.
- I2C-HID: Intel LPSS DesignWare I2C controller with HID-over-I2C touchpads,
  found through ACPI (experimental).
- Input interface: evdev (/dev/input/event*), used by the X server.
- Display: Intel i915, VMware SVGA II/SVGA3D (vmwgfx), UEFI GOP framebuffer,
  /dev/fb0 (fbdev).
- Backlight: Intel PWM backlight in /sys/class/backlight/intel_backlight and
  the backlight tool.
- Sound and network: see the next sections.

Sound
~~~~~
LikeOS has its own sound stack, from the kernel drivers up to the desktop.

Kernel drivers:

- Intel High Definition Audio: HD Audio controllers from Intel's ICH6 through
  current platforms, NVIDIA, AMD/ATI and other vendors.  A generic codec
  parser works from the board's pin configuration, with codec drivers for
  Realtek, Conexant, SigmaTel/IDT, Analog Devices SoundMAX, Cirrus Logic, VIA
  and Creative CA0132, and for the emulated codecs of QEMU and other
  hypervisors.  Headphone auto-mute and jack detection.
- HDMI and DisplayPort audio: the display codecs of Intel, NVIDIA and AMD
  graphics; monitors are identified from their ELD; on Intel graphics the i915
  display driver and the sound driver work together.
- Intel audio DSP: cAVS 2.5 and ACE platforms with SOF IPC4 firmware and
  topology, the Intel SoundWire controller (Cannon Lake to Panther Lake) and
  the Cirrus Logic CS42L43 jack codec of recent laptops.
- AC'97: Intel ICH AC'97 controllers and register-compatible parts, with AC'97
  codecs.
- Ensoniq AudioPCI: ES1370, ES1371, ES1373, Creative CT5880 ("Sound Blaster
  PCI 64/128") -- also the sound card VMware and QEMU emulate.
- Device interface: /dev/audioN for playback and recording with jack and
  monitor events, plus OSS-compatible /dev/dsp and /dev/mixer; owned by root
  and the group audio.

The sound service:

- audiod owns the sound card and mixes the sound of every program into one
  stream, so a video in the browser, the media player and a notification sound
  can play at the same time, each at its own volume.
- Programs send sound in whatever format they have -- 8 to 32-bit integer or
  32/64-bit float, 1 to 8 channels, 4 kHz to 384 kHz.  The service resamples
  with an exact-ratio windowed-sinc filter, maps the channels to the card,
  ramps volume changes so they never click, and limits the sum softly so that
  many loud streams do not distort.
- Headphone and line jacks and HDMI/DisplayPort monitors are followed as they
  come and go; when the monitor that sound was sent to goes away, it falls back
  to the speakers and returns once the monitor is back.
- After a few seconds of silence the card is stopped, so an idle system has no
  wake-ups for sound.  Volume, mute and the chosen output survive restarts.
- Programs connect through libaudio; GStreamer's likeosaudiosink is picked
  automatically, so WebKit and the media player play through the service.

On the desktop and in the terminal:

- A volume control in the taskbar: the speaker opens a slider with a mute
  button; the mouse wheel over the speaker changes the volume, and a middle
  click mutes.
- Media Player for music and video: WAV, AIFF, AU, AAC/M4A, MP3, Ogg Vorbis,
  Opus and FLAC; MP4, Matroska and WebM video with H.264, H.265, VP8 and VP9;
  internet streams including HLS and DASH; a play queue and M3U/PLS playlists.
- System sounds when the desktop starts and when you leave it, from a sound
  set synthesized for LikeOS.
- Command-line tools: audioplay (WAV, AIFF, AU, CAF and raw PCM), audioctl
  (volume, mute, outputs and streams), audiotest (test signals and latency),
  audioinfo (what the sound hardware can do) and eventsound.

Networking
~~~~~~~~~~
- Ethernet, ARP, IPv4, ICMP, IGMP, UDP and TCP, with a routing table and
  softirq-based receive processing.  The stack is IPv4-only.
- In-kernel DHCP client and DNS resolver (forward and reverse).
- BSD socket API: sendmsg/recvmsg, accept4, sendfile, shutdown half-close, raw
  sockets for ping and traceroute.
- Socket options including SO_REUSEADDR, SO_REUSEPORT, SO_KEEPALIVE,
  SO_LINGER, buffer sizes and timeouts, TCP_NODELAY and the keepalive
  tunables.
- TCP: window scaling and timestamps (RFC 7323), selective acknowledgements
  (RFC 2018), NewReno congestion control (RFC 5681), RTO computation
  (RFC 6298), initial window of ten segments (RFC 6928), SYN cookies
  (RFC 4987), SipHash-based initial sequence numbers (RFC 6528), challenge
  ACKs (RFC 5961), path-MTU handling (RFC 1191).
- Unix domain sockets: stream, datagram and sequenced-packet sockets;
  filesystem and abstract names; file-descriptor passing (SCM_RIGHTS) and peer
  credentials (SO_PEERCRED) -- what D-Bus, the X server and DRI3 rely on.
- Network drivers:

    e1000      Intel 82540EM, 82545EM (QEMU, VMware default, VirtualBox
               PRO/1000)
    e1000e     Intel 82574L, 82583V, I217, I218 and the I219 family
               (generations 1-23); verified on the ThinkPad P50
    igb        Intel 82575, 82576, I350, I210, I211
    eepro100   Intel 8255x
    pcnet32    AMD PCnet-PCI II / PCnet-FAST III
    ne2k       NE2000-compatible PCI (Realtek RTL8029AS)
    rtl8139    Realtek RTL8139 / RTL8139C+
    tulip      DEC 21040, 21041, 21140, 21142, 21143
    vmxnet3    VMware paravirtual NIC (ESXi, Workstation, Fusion, QEMU)

- Command-line tools: ifconfig, route, arp, arping, ping, traceroute, netstat,
  dhclient, host, dig, nslookup, hostname, plus the ported curl, netcat and
  OpenSSH.

Graphics and GPU drivers
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
DRM/KMS core -- a display-manager core written for LikeOS, offering the
standard DRM ioctl interface that libdrm, Mesa and the X server's modesetting
driver use:

- Primary nodes (/dev/dri/card*) and render nodes (/dev/dri/renderD*), DRM
  master handling.
- Atomic and legacy mode setting: CRTCs, planes (primary, overlay, cursor),
  connectors, properties, blending, colour management and damage clips.
- Vblank counters and events, page flips, CRTC sequence queries.
- GEM buffer objects, dumb buffers, and PRIME/DMA-BUF sharing between
  processes.
- Fences, fence arrays and chains, reservation objects, sync files, and binary
  and timeline sync objects.
- Dirty tracking of coherently mapped buffers through the page tables.
- EDID, DisplayID and ELD parsing; DisplayPort, dual-mode, DSC, HDMI infoframe
  and SCDC helpers.
- Device suspend/resume with a gate that holds clients off the hardware.
- The kernel console becomes a DRM client when a driver is present, and hands
  the screen to the X server and takes it back.

Intel i915 driver -- a native driver whose device table covers Intel graphics
from the i830 (generation 2) through Xe3 (Lunar Lake, Battlemage, Panther
Lake, Wildcat Lake, Nova Lake).  The Skylake-class HD Graphics 530 of the
ThinkPad P50 is the reference machine.

- Command submission: ring buffers on generations 2-7; execution lists with
  per-context 48-bit address spaces from Broadwell; GuC firmware submission
  (interface version 70) on the parts that require it (DG2, Meteor Lake and
  later), including the separate media GT.
- Engines: render, blitter, video decode, video enhancement and compute.
- Global GTT, per-process GTT (4-level, 5-level on Xe2+), stolen memory, and
  device-local memory on the discrete DG1, DG2 and Battlemage cards.
- Render P-states (RPS), and SLPC frequency management through the GuC.
- Firmware: DMC display microcode, GuC and HuC images from /lib/firmware/i915
  and /lib/firmware/xe.
- Hang detection and engine reset; workarounds and MOCS tables per platform.
- The i915 uAPI Mesa's iris driver and the VA-API media drivers use: GEM
  objects, contexts and VMs, execbuffer2 with soft-pin and sync objects,
  queries.

Intel display engine:

- Embedded DisplayPort panels from the VBT and EDID: link training, panel
  power sequencing, PWM backlight, ACPI OpRegion.
- DisplayPort, HDMI and DVI outputs with hotplug detection; Type-C ports (Ice
  Lake to Meteor Lake).
- Display stream compression (DSC), variable refresh timing, colour
  management, X- and Y-tiled scan-out.
- PLLs and PHYs across generations: combo PHYs, Synopsys PHYs (DG2), C10/C20
  PHYs (Meteor Lake and Arrow Lake), LT PHY (Nova Lake).
- Legacy outputs of older generations: VGA, LVDS, SDVO, DVO, the TV encoder,
  and MIPI DSI panels on Bay Trail and Cherry Trail.
- Display bandwidth, watermarks, CDCLK and power-well management.

VMware SVGA (vmwgfx):

- A hardware layer for the SVGA II adapter found in VMware and VirtualBox
  (VMSVGA) -- not used under QEMU, where LikeOS draws on the UEFI framebuffer;
  every optional feature is gated on what the host advertises.
- Three scan-out paths chosen by device capability: legacy VRAM, screen
  objects and screen targets.
- SVGA3D with guest-backed objects (MOBs, GB surfaces), DX (VGPU10) contexts,
  views and shaders, and command buffers.
- Command-stream validation and handle translation, so no process can name
  another's objects.
- FIFO and interrupt-driven fences, a hardware cursor, and the video overlay
  stream offered to X as an XVideo adaptor.
- Suspend/resume, and the guest-to-host message channel.

Userspace graphics stack:

    Mesa 25.3.6          Gallium drivers: iris (Intel, Broadwell and later),
                         svga (VMware/VirtualBox SVGA3D), llvmpipe, softpipe.
                         OpenGL, OpenGL ES 2, GLX (DRI), EGL and GBM on X11
    LLVM 23.1.0          built for LikeOS (x86 back end) as llvmpipe's JIT
    libdrm 2.4.124       DRM userspace interface
    libepoxy 1.5.10      GL dispatch for GTK
    GLU 9.0.3, Mesa demos 9.0.0

The X server runs glamor (OpenGL-accelerated 2D), DRI3 and Present on Intel
graphics and on VMware or VirtualBox with 3D enabled; clients such as WebKit
render on the GPU through the render node.  Elsewhere it draws in software and
GL falls back to llvmpipe.  drminfo reports the driver and its capabilities.

Desktop and applications
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
Session:

- startx from any account: the X server is installed setuid root and drops
  privileges once its devices are open.
- The server configuration is chosen at start-up from the detected GPU (i915,
  VMware 3D, other DRM driver, or plain fbdev).
- A D-Bus session bus is started with the session.
- A start-up sound when the desktop comes up, and a sound when you leave it.

Windows 11-style look:

- ctwm window manager with LikeOS additions: flat, 30-pixel "Fluent" title
  bars with the application icon, wide minimise/maximise/close buttons (close
  in red), anti-aliased Xft title fonts, click-to-focus and raise-on-click.
- taskbar, a LikeOS GTK panel along the bottom: start button with a menu built
  from the installed .desktop files, a button per window with focus highlight
  and running indicators, a four-workspace switcher, a load and memory
  monitor, a volume control and a clock -- driven purely through the EWMH and
  ICCCM standards.
- A LikeOS GTK 3 theme compiled from SCSS, Windows-style message dialogs, and
  the LikeOS cursor theme generated at build time.
- Fonts: Selawik as the interface font, plus Noto Sans, DejaVu, Liberation and
  the X core fonts.
- PCManFM draws the wallpaper and desktop icons; new accounts receive
  launchers in ~/Desktop.

X Window System and toolkits:

    X.Org server 21.1.23          modesetting driver with glamor, DRI2/DRI3,
                                  Present, GLX; XVideo
    xf86-video-fbdev 0.5.0,       software fallback and input
      xf86-input-evdev 2.11.0
    libX11 1.8.13, libxcb 1.17.0  plus the usual X extension libraries
    libxkbcommon 1.13.1,          keyboard handling
      xkeyboard-config 2.47
    FreeType 2.13.3, fontconfig   font rendering and pixel compositing
      2.15.0, pixman 0.46.4
    Motif 2.3.8                   toolkit for XNEdit
    GTK 3.24.52                   main toolkit; GTK 2.24.33 is also built for
                                  HexChat
    GLib 2.88.3                   GObject, GIO; glib-networking 2.80.1
    Cairo 1.18.4, Pango 1.58.0,   with FriBidi 1.0.16
      HarfBuzz 14.3.0
    gdk-pixbuf 2.44.7, ATK 2.38.0
    GtkSourceView 4.8.4,          editor and terminal widgets
      VTE 0.84.1
    D-Bus 1.16.2                  session bus
    libstdc++ (GCC 13.3.0)        C++ runtime for the C++ ports
    Adwaita 3.38.0, hicolor 0.18,
      shared-mime-info 2.5.1

Applications:

    ctwm 4.1.0              window manager (with LikeOS patches)
    PCManFM 1.4.0           file manager and desktop (libfm 1.4.1,
                            menu-cache 1.1.1)
    Claws Mail 4.4.0        IMAP, POP3 and SMTP over TLS (libetpan,
                            GnuTLS 3.8.13), spell checking via Enchant and
                            Hunspell
    Mousepad 0.7.0          GTK text editor
    xfce4-terminal 1.2.0    VTE-based terminal (libxfce4ui 4.20.2,
                            xfconf 4.20.0)
    HexChat 2.16.2          IRC client
    galculator 2.1.4        calculator
    Media Player 1.0        LikeOS music and video player on GStreamer
    luakit 2.4.0            web browser
    xterm 410               UTF-8, 256 colours
    XNEdit 1.6.3            Motif text editor
    X clients               xcalc, xclock, xload, xsetroot, xrandr, xset,
                            xauth, xvinfo, twm

Web browser and multimedia
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
WebKitGTK 2.52.6:

- The GTK 3 API (webkit2gtk-4.1) on the X11 target, with feature options
  matching a mainstream desktop distribution build.
- Skia rendering with its GPU back end on Mesa, a GPU process, and frames
  passed between processes as DMA-BUF descriptors via GBM; CPU rendering where
  there is no GPU.
- JavaScriptCore with all JIT tiers, WebAssembly and the sampling profiler.
- GStreamer media: video, Web Audio, Media Source Extensions, MediaStream,
  MediaRecorder, WebCodecs and GStreamer GL.
- Gamepads (libmanette), WebDriver, colour management (LCMS), hyphenation,
  libsecret, MiniBrowser.
- Networking through libsoup 3.6.6 with GnuTLS, HTTP/2 (nghttp2), the public
  suffix list (libpsl); ICU 78.3, SQLite, WOFF2/Brotli and WebP.

luakit 2.4.0:

- Keyboard-driven browser scripted in Lua 5.1.5 (with LuaFileSystem 1.9.0).
- Hardware acceleration always on; window size chosen from the screen size.
- Sound from web pages through the system sound service, with Web Audio on;
  videos start only when clicked.
- Built-in ad blocking with option-aware filter rules and a keyword index,
  shipping EasyList, EasyPrivacy, Peter Lowe's list and more than twenty
  regional lists.
- An optional launcher keeps the browser's profile and caches in RAM (/ram).
- A cheat sheet of its key bindings and of the LikeOS-specific settings is on
  the web site: https://likeos.systemtrap.com/luakit.html

Multimedia:

    GStreamer 1.24.13          core with base, good, bad and libav plugins
    FFmpeg 7.1.1               codecs (H.264, H.265, VP8, VP9; AAC, MP3,
                               Vorbis, Opus, FLAC, ALAC), ffmpeg/ffprobe,
                               VA-API hardware acceleration
    likeosaudiosink 1.0        GStreamer sound output to the audiod service,
                               chosen automatically
    libva / libva-utils 2.22.0 VA-API, vainfo
    Intel media driver (iHD)   24.3.4, with gmmlib 22.5.2
    Intel VA-API driver (i965) 2.4.1, fallback driver

On Intel graphics, video is decoded on the GPU's video engines through VA-API
(GStreamer's va decoders for H.264, H.265, VP8, VP9 and AV1, and FFmpeg's
VA-API hwaccels).  On VMware, the adapter's overlay is offered as the "VMware
Overlay Video Engine" XVideo adaptor next to glamor's textured adaptor.

C library and runtime
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
LikeOS has its own C library (libc.so and libc.a) and its own dynamic linker,
ld-likeos.so, both written for the system.

- Dynamic linker: dlopen/dlsym, GNU hash tables, static and dynamic TLS
  (__tls_get_addr), constructors and DT_INIT_ARRAY, RELRO.
- Threads: POSIX threads (mutexes including robust ones, condition variables,
  barriers, read-write locks, thread-specific data), POSIX semaphores, and C11
  <threads.h>.
- malloc: bin-based allocator with per-thread caches and multiple arenas.
- Math: correctly rounded double and float functions from LLVM libc, plus
  fenv.
- Unicode and locales: UTF-8 locale, the full multibyte and wide-character
  interface, wcwidth, <uchar.h>, generated Unicode tables.
- iconv: UTF-8, UTF-16, UTF-32, ISO-8859-1, ISO-8859-15 and ASCII.
- POSIX regular expressions (BRE and ERE, leftmost-longest), fnmatch, glob,
  ftw.
- Time zones from POSIX TZ rules or common Olson names.
- Networking: getaddrinfo, resolver, getifaddrs; users and groups: passwd,
  group, shadow.
- posix_spawn built on clone(CLONE_VM|CLONE_VFORK), popen,
  makecontext/swapcontext, PTY helpers, termios, System V IPC.
- Stack-smashing protection in libc and in every program built against it.

Base system ports:

    bash 5.2.37        the system shell, installed as /bin/sh
    OpenSSH 10.4p1     client tools and the sshd server
    OpenSSL 3.5.6      TLS and crypto library and toolkit
    curl 8.14.1        with nghttp2 1.65.0
    tmux 3.6a          terminal multiplexer (libevent 2.1.12)
    nano 8.3           text editor with syntax highlighting
    netcat (OpenBSD)   networking utility
    zlib 1.3.1         compression
    ncurses API        a LikeOS implementation of the curses interface
    GDB 17.2           debugger and gdbserver

Core utilities, written for LikeOS: ls, cat, cp, mv, rm, mkdir, rmdir, ln,
touch, chmod, stat, file, find, readlink, cmp, grep, sed, expr, wc, sort, uniq,
cut, tr, head, tail, more, less, strings, hexdump, uname, uptime, date, time,
ps, top, df, du, free, dmesg, nproc, kill, sleep, env, which, echo, printf,
man, tty, shutdown, poweroff, reboot, halt and more.

Security and users
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
- Multi-user login: init -> getty -> login, driven by /etc/inittab.
- PAM with a yescrypt-based crypt(3); /etc/passwd, /etc/shadow, /etc/group.
- Real, effective and saved user and group IDs, supplementary groups and
  set-user-ID execution.
- Discretionary access control enforced in the VFS through a single privilege
  check, so permissions apply to every path that opens a file.
- Device permissions by group (audio, video, render); /proc/<pid>/mem and
  process maps restricted to the owner and root.
- ptrace and process inspection subject to the same access check.
- SMEP and SMAP where the CPU supports them, NX mappings, ring 3 isolation.
- Stack canaries in the kernel and userland; RELRO in the dynamic linker.
- ChaCha20 CSPRNG, SYN cookies and SipHash TCP sequence numbers.
- chroot; OpenSSH server for remote logins.
- Account tools: id, whoami, groups, su, passwd, adduser, addgroup, deluser,
  delgroup.

Developer tools
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
- GDB 17.2 and gdbserver running on LikeOS, on top of the kernel's ptrace with
  hardware watchpoints.
- User crash reports that name the faulting mapping and offset and recover a
  probable call chain, resolvable with addr2line even for stripped libraries.
- Kernel crash dumps and the assertion framework; debug builds with symbols
  and memory poisoning.
- Diagnostics: memstat (system and per-process memory accounting), pmap (one
  process's address space, with a watch mode), kdump (the kernel's TCP,
  AF_UNIX, PTY and task tables), ps with WCHAN, dmesg, drminfo.
- On-target test programs: system-call, libc, memory, fork, network, OpenSSL
  and ext4 tests.
- Host-side unit tests for libc (malloc, printf, strings, math, time zones,
  Unicode) and for the DRM and i915 code (EDID, VBT, DisplayPort, DSC, fences,
  sync objects, atomic checks, watermarks, colour, PLLs).

Building LikeOS
---------------

Everything is built from source on a build host by the top-level Makefile,
including the third-party ports under ports/.  The result is a single file,
build/likeos-ext4.img, a complete bootable GPT disk:

  Partition 1   a small FAT EFI System Partition (64 MB by default) that holds
                only the UEFI bootloader, /EFI/BOOT/BOOTX64.EFI
  Partition 2   the ext4 root filesystem with the kernel (/boot/kernel.elf)
                and the whole userland; the bootloader reads the kernel
                straight out of ext4

The short version:

  git clone https://github.com/kctherootkey/LikeOS-64 LikeOS
  cd LikeOS
  git checkout dev            # optional: unstable, probably with more features
  sudo apt-get update
  sudo make deps
  make
  lsblk
  make usb-write USB_DEVICE=/dev/sdX

The full, step-by-step guide is on the web site:
https://likeos.systemtrap.com/running.html

Requirements
~~~~~~~~~~~~
- Build host: an x86-64 machine running Ubuntu.  Building has been tested with
  Ubuntu 24.04.3 LTS (Noble Numbat).  Ubuntu is currently required; other
  distributions are likely to need work on the dependency list and on the host
  tools the ports drive.
- Privileges: an account with sudo; installing the dependencies and writing
  the USB stick both need it.  The build itself runs as your normal user (the
  root filesystem image is populated under fakeroot).
- Disk space: at least 40 GB free.  A fully built tree occupies roughly 26 GB.
- Memory: 8 GB of RAM is the practical minimum; more is better.  The WebKitGTK
  build sizes its parallelism from your RAM, and the final link of
  libWebKit.so is the high-water mark of the whole build.  Some swap space is a
  sensible safety net.
- CPU time: the first full build takes several hours.  Later builds are
  incremental: every port package leaves a stamp, so a re-run continues where
  it left off and only rebuilds what changed.
- Internet access: to clone the repository (its git history is roughly three
  quarters of a gigabyte, because most third-party source tarballs are checked
  in), to install the Ubuntu packages, and for the ports' fetch.sh scripts,
  which download any source tarball not in the repository and verify every
  archive against a recorded SHA-256 checksum.
- USB stick: at least 4 GB; 8 GB or more is recommended (the image is about
  1.8 GB, and writing it grows the root filesystem to fill the stick).  A USB 3
  stick makes a noticeable difference.  The stick is completely erased.
- Target machine: an x86-64 PC with UEFI firmware, or a virtual machine in
  VMware or VirtualBox; QEMU works too, through legacy targets.

Getting the source
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
  sudo apt-get install git
  git clone https://github.com/kctherootkey/LikeOS-64 LikeOS
  cd LikeOS

The clone starts on master, the stable branch.  Optionally, check out the
unstable dev branch instead, which probably has more features:

  git checkout dev

Later, git pull on the branch you are on followed by make brings your build up
to date; only what changed is rebuilt.

Installing the build dependencies
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
  sudo apt-get update
  sudo make deps

make deps is the requirement -- do not install the packages by hand.  Besides
the compiler and the image tools (gcc, nasm, GNU-EFI, mtools, dosfstools,
e2fsprogs, fakeroot, gdisk/parted, QEMU with OVMF) it installs what the X.Org
and GTK3 ports need on the host (meson, ninja, cmake, libtool, gperf, xsltproc,
bison, flex, xfonts-utils, shared-mime-info, gettext, the DocBook stylesheets,
zstd and ruby), LLVM and clang with libclc and the SPIR-V tools for Mesa's
Intel driver, and a new enough g++ for the C++ ports.  Where the distribution's
meson is too old for GLib, a current one is installed into a Python virtual
environment inside the port (ports/xorg/.hosttools), not system-wide.

If make deps stops with "python3 -m venv failed":

  sudo apt-get install python3-venv
  sudo make deps

Building
~~~~~~~~
  make

make and make all are the same thing.  It builds the UEFI bootloader
(build/bootloader.efi), the kernel (build/kernel.elf), the C library, the
dynamic linker, PAM and crypt libraries and the user programs, the smaller
ports (ncurses, bash, nano, tmux, libevent, zlib, nghttp2, OpenSSL, curl,
OpenSSH, netcat and gdb), the X.Org port (make ports-xorg) and the GTK3 port
(make ports-gtk3: GLib, GTK, Cairo, Pango, HarfBuzz, libstdc++, LLVM and Mesa,
GStreamer and FFmpeg, libva and the Intel media drivers, WebKitGTK, luakit,
PCManFM, Claws Mail, Mousepad, xfce4-terminal and the rest) into the shared
sysroot build/xorg-sysroot, and finally stages everything into
build/likeos-ext4.img.

Because the first build runs for hours, keep a log and run it somewhere that
survives a closed terminal (inside tmux, for example):

  make 2>&1 | tee build.log

If the build is interrupted, simply run make again.

Make targets (make help prints the complete list):

  make all                  everything, ending in build/likeos-ext4.img
  make usb                  alias for the image; same result as all
  make kernel               only build/kernel.elf
  make bootloader           only build/bootloader.efi
  make ports-xorg           fetch, unpack and build the X.Org port
  make ports-gtk3           fetch, unpack and build the GTK3 port
  make usb-write USB_DEVICE=/dev/sdX
                            build the image if it is not current, write it to
                            a USB stick and grow the root filesystem to fill it
  make qemu, make qemu-usb  legacy: boot the image file in QEMU
  make qemu-realusb USB_DEVICE=/dev/sdX
                            boot a stick written by usb-write in QEMU (console
                            only)
  make clean                remove build output but keep the ports and the
                            sysroot, for a fast incremental rebuild
  make distclean            clean everything, ports included (downloaded
                            tarballs are kept)
  make deps                 install the build dependencies
  make help                 every target and build variable

Build variables, passed on the make command line (they can be combined):

  SCREEN_SIZE=medium        prefer 1280x800 on real hardware instead of the
                            large default (see "Screen resolution")
  MAX_SCREEN_SIZE=WxH       a ceiling on that list, e.g. 1920x1080
  LIKEOS_VERSION=...        version string shown in the boot banner and by
                            uname (default 0.2.7-HEAD)
  DEBUG=1                   full debug build: kernel memory poisoning, DWARF
                            symbols, unstripped kernel and userspace, detailed
                            crash reports
  CRASH_VERBOSE=1           detailed kernel and userspace crash reports in an
                            otherwise normal, stripped build
  NO_STRIP=1                keep the kernel's symbol table
  NO_STRIP_USER=1           keep symbol tables in userspace, for gdb on LikeOS
  SERIAL=1                  mirror the kernel log to COM1 (the QEMU targets
                            show it on the terminal)
  USB_SERIAL=1              mirror the kernel log to a USB CDC ACM serial
                            device on real hardware
  I915_DEBUG=1              detailed log from the Intel graphics driver
  EXT4_MB=..., ESP_MB=...   force the root / EFI System Partition sizes in MB
  LIKEOS_WEBKIT_JOBS=N      (environment) number of parallel WebKitGTK compile
                            jobs, overriding the memory-based estimate
  QEMU_SOUND, QEMU_AUDIODEV, QEMU_MEM, NUM_CPUS, NO_SMP, USB_HID, NIC_DEVICE
                            QEMU targets only: sound card and host back end,
                            memory, CPUs, USB keyboard/mouse, network card

Changing options needs no clean.  SCREEN_SIZE and MAX_SCREEN_SIZE rebuild just
the bootloader and the display drivers; switching DEBUG or CRASH_VERBOSE
removes the kernel's objects automatically.

Rebuilding a single port package: the X.Org and GTK3 ports are driven by
build.sh scripts that rebuild just the packages named (-f ignores the stamps);
run make afterwards to put the result into the image:

  ports/xorg/build.sh libX11 libXext
  ports/xorg/gtk3/build.sh claws-mail
  ports/xorg/gtk3/build.sh -f webkitgtk
  make

Package names are the ones in ports/xorg/packages.list and
ports/xorg/gtk3/packages.list; each package's log is in ports/xorg/.logs/ or
ports/xorg/gtk3/.logs/.  The smaller ports have their own targets, such as
make ports-bash, make ports-openssh or make ports-curl.  ports/xorg/clean.sh
and ports/xorg/gtk3/clean.sh return a port to its checked-in state.

If the build host is also your workstation, cap the memory the WebKitGTK build
may use with a systemd scope:

  systemd-run --user --scope -p MemoryHigh=4000M -p MemoryMax=5000M \
      ports/xorg/gtk3/build.sh webkitgtk

Writing the USB stick
---------------------

This erases the whole target device.  Identify the stick with lsblk before and
after plugging it in, name the whole disk (/dev/sdb, not /dev/sdb1), and read
the device name twice before you press Enter.

  lsblk -d -o NAME,SIZE,MODEL,TRAN
  make usb-write USB_DEVICE=/dev/sdX

The target builds the image if it is not current, warns and waits for Enter,
writes the image with dd, and then grows the ext4 root partition and
filesystem to fill the whole stick.  When it reports "UEFI bootable USB drive
created successfully", the stick is ready.  If it says the stick did not grow,
unplug and replug it and finish by hand:

  sudo e2fsck -fy /dev/sdX2
  sudo resize2fs /dev/sdX2

Booting LikeOS
--------------

The supported way to run LikeOS is from a written stick: on real hardware, or
in VMware or VirtualBox with the stick attached to the virtual machine.

Real hardware: boot in UEFI mode with Secure Boot disabled (the bootloader is
not signed), and choose the stick's UEFI entry in the firmware's one-time boot
menu (usually F12 on ThinkPad and Dell notebooks).  After the boot messages you
get a login prompt on the console.  Networking is wired Ethernet; the kernel
requests an address over DHCP while it boots.

VMware: create a 64-bit virtual machine without a virtual hard disk, set the
firmware type to UEFI and the USB controller's compatibility to USB 3.1.
Accelerate 3D graphics on lets X and GL clients (the browser included) render
on the virtual GPU; off, LikeOS renders in software -- both work.  The e1000,
e1000e and vmxnet3 adapters all have drivers; keep the sound card (Sound
Blaster PCI, es1371).  Connect the stick to the virtual machine and power it
on.

VirtualBox: create a 64-bit virtual machine without a hard disk and enable
EFI.  Choose the VMSVGA graphics controller (enable 3D acceleration to use the
virtual GPU), the USB 3.0 (xHCI) controller with a device filter for your
stick, the ICH AC97 audio controller, and an Intel PRO/1000 (e1000) or PCnet
network adapter.

QEMU (legacy targets):

  make qemu
  make qemu-usb
  make qemu-realusb USB_DEVICE=/dev/sdX

These use OVMF, an xHCI controller, an e1000 card with user-mode networking
and an Intel HD Audio controller.  LikeOS draws on the UEFI framebuffer there,
without GPU acceleration.  make qemu boots build/likeos-ext4.img as a USB
stick; make qemu-usb does the same through sudo, so that ping to outside hosts
works; make qemu-realusb boots a stick written by usb-write and is console
only -- do not run startx under it.  The guest gets 1024 MB by default; raise
it with QEMU_MEM=2G.

To run X from a real stick under QEMU, drive it through virt-manager with KVM.
This configuration is known to work:

  1.  Create a new virtual machine in virt-manager; select manual installation.
  2.  Choose Debian 13 as the operating system.
  3.  Give it 2048 MB of RAM and, for example, 4 CPUs.
  4.  Tick "Customize configuration before install".
  5.  Under Overview, set the firmware to UEFI with OVMF_CODE_4M.fd.
  6.  For the NIC, select Bridge and enter your bridge device (e.g. virbr0).
  7.  Select e1000e as the NIC model first, then change it to e1000 in the XML.
  8.  Set Video to VGA.
  9.  Boot the machine, connect the USB stick to it, and type exit at the UEFI
      prompt.  LikeOS then boots from the stick.

Graphics is chosen automatically when X starts: its start-up scripts ask the
display driver what it is with drminfo and pick the configuration to match.

  Intel graphics (i915)           xorg.conf.modesetting-i915: glamor, DRI3,
                                  page flipping; GPU rendering through iris,
                                  video decoding through VA-API
  VMware / VirtualBox, 3D on      xorg.conf.modesetting-vmwgfx-3d: glamor;
                                  GPU rendering through Mesa's svga driver
  3D off, or another DRM driver   xorg.conf.modesetting; software (llvmpipe)
  No display driver at all        xorg.conf: fbdev on /dev/fb0; software

First steps after boot
----------------------

Log in as root at the console:

  login: root
  password: toor

Change the root password straight away with passwd, then create an ordinary
account -- LikeOS is multi-user and file permissions are enforced, and the
desktop is meant to be used from an ordinary account.  adduser creates the home
directory under /home and copies the desktop launchers into it from /etc/skel:

  passwd
  adduser alice
  exit

Log in as the new user and start the graphical desktop:

  startx

Any user can run startx: the X server is installed setuid root and gives root
up as soon as it has opened the display and input devices, so no group
membership is needed.

The desktop: X starts with the ctwm window manager, the PCManFM desktop
(wallpaper and icons) and the LikeOS taskbar.

- Desktop icons: Files (PCManFM), Terminal (xfce4-terminal), Xterm, Luakit Web
  Browser, Mail (Claws Mail), Text Editor (Mousepad), XNEdit, Calculator
  (galculator), X Calculator, Hexchat IRC Client and Media Player.
- Taskbar: the load and memory monitor on the left; the start button and one
  button per open window in the middle; the four workspace buttons, the volume
  control and the clock with the date on the right.
- Start menu: Programs, built from the launchers on your desktop; Settings >
  Display; Restart session and Exit session; Reboot and Poweroff.
- Keyboard menus: all three mouse buttons over the desktop belong to PCManFM,
  so the window manager's menus are on the keyboard.  Alt+F1 opens the root
  menu, Alt+F2 the window operations menu.

From a terminal:

  luakit &
  claws-mail &
  xfce4-terminal &
  pcmanfm &
  mousepad &
  mediaplayer &

Sound works without any setup: audiod starts at boot and the desktop greets
you with its start-up sound.  Every account has a sample video in ~/Videos.

  audioctl volume 60
  audioplay /usr/share/sounds/likeos/startup.wav
  audioinfo

Check the network from a terminal before you start the browser or mail client:

  ifconfig
  ping -c 3 example.com

Leaving the desktop: choose Exit session in the start menu or the Alt+F1 menu;
you return to the console.

Shutting down and rebooting: Reboot and Poweroff in the start menu, or
poweroff, reboot, shutdown -h now and shutdown -r now in a terminal.  Root and
the user logged in at the machine's own console may use them; from any other
session (an SSH login, for example) become root first with su.  shutdown also
accepts a delay (+m minutes, or hh:mm); halt stops the machine without powering
it off.

Screen resolution
-----------------

Settings > Display in the start menu (likeos-display-settings) lists the sizes
the screen can be set to.  With a display driver (Intel or VMware/VirtualBox
SVGA) the size changes at once and you are asked whether to keep it; nobody
answering within 20 seconds switches back.  On the boot framebuffer the chosen
size applies from the next start.  The choice is saved in
/etc/likeos/display.conf and used everywhere: by the bootloader, the kernel's
display drivers and the X server.

Without a saved size:

- Virtual machines start at 1280x800.
- Real hardware takes the first size the firmware offers from a build-time
  list.  A default build tries every standard size above 1920x1200 first, then
  1920x1200, 1920x1080, 1280x800, 1280x768 and 1024x768; make
  SCREEN_SIZE=medium starts at 1280x800.  make MAX_SCREEN_SIZE=1920x1080 puts
  a ceiling on the size the machine starts in on its own: anything above it
  is skipped.  A size saved in Display settings is not held to it.

Troubleshooting
---------------

- The build stops at GLib with "Meson version is 1.3.2 but project requires
  >= 1.4.0": run sudo make deps again.
- A port package fails to build: look at its log in ports/xorg/.logs/ or
  ports/xorg/gtk3/.logs/; after fixing it, make continues from that package.
- The WebKitGTK build dies with "ninja: build stopped", or the host swaps
  heavily: that is usually memory.  Run LIKEOS_WEBKIT_JOBS=1 make.
- The build says the X server or the GTK3 stack is missing although the stamps
  say it was built: the sysroot was removed under the stamps.  Run
  ports/xorg/clean.sh && make.
- The machine does not boot from the stick: check UEFI mode, Secure Boot off,
  and the stick's UEFI entry; in a VM, that the firmware is UEFI/EFI and the
  stick is attached.
- Which graphics path am I on?  su -c drminfo reports the display driver, its
  outputs and capabilities (drminfo --driver just the name).  The X server log
  is /var/log/Xorg.0.log.
- Logs and crash reports: dmesg shows the kernel log; rebuild with
  CRASH_VERBOSE=1, DEBUG=1, SERIAL=1, USB_SERIAL=1 or I915_DEBUG=1 for more.

Reporting problems: please report bugs and build failures on GitHub, or write
to the author at niko@systemtrap.com.  Say which machine or virtual machine you
used (and, for a VM, whether 3D acceleration was on), which commit you built,
and include the crash report, the relevant lines of dmesg, or the failing
port's log.

Project structure
-----------------

- boot/: UEFI bootloader and trampoline.
- kernel/: the kernel, by subsystem (ke, hal, mm, fs, io, dev, net), each
  header beside the sources it belongs to (kernel/ke/sched.h next to
  kernel/ke/sched.c); kernel/uapi/ holds the interfaces shared with user
  space, kernel/dev/video/gpu/ the display drivers (drm, i915, vmwgfx).
- user/bin/: user programs (core utilities, the desktop shell programs and
  tests).
- user/lib/libc/: C library and runtime pieces.
- user/lib/rtld/: dynamic linker.
- res/: files staged into the image (configuration, fonts, wallpaper, desktop
  launchers in /etc/skel).
- host/: build-host tools (table generators and the host-side tests).
- ports/: third-party software -- ports/xorg for the X Window System and
  ports/xorg/gtk3 for GTK3 and the applications on it.  Upstream sources are
  kept byte-identical; LikeOS changes live as patches and build files beside
  each port.

Planned work
------------

- Storage drivers: AHCI/SATA and NVMe.
- Mounting removable media.
- Expanded POSIX coverage.
- Package management.
- Pre-built images, with the first major release.

